   "reasoning_summary": "C5H10O has DoU 1. No carbonyl or sp2 carbon is seen, so the molecule contains a ring. Only three 13C signals (73.7, 35.3, 23.1) are observed, which requires a symmetric molecule. The carbon at 73.7 indicates a C-O carbon, and the 1H at 4.26 is typical of a secondary carbinol CH. The 13C set matches cyclopentanol almost exactly (73.9/35.5/23.3 in the literature). Peak 2 at 2.36 is assigned to the exchangeable OH, and peaks 3 and 4 to the eight ring CH2 protons. The listed integrals (2/1/3/4) depart from the ideal 1/1/4/4 by about 1H, which is attributed to integration error. Tetrahydropyran and 3,3-dimethyloxetane are weaker alternatives because their shifts mismatch the data."
